DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggppaarrtt utility is used to partition GEOM providers, normally disks.
     The first argument is the action to be taken:

     aadddd       Add a new partition to the partitioning scheme given by _g_e_o_m.
               The partition type must be specified with --tt _t_y_p_e.  The parti‐
               tion's location, size, and other attributes will be calculated
               automatically if the corresponding options are not specified.

               The aadddd command accepts these options:

               --aa _a_l_i_g_n_m_e_n_t  If specified, then the ggppaarrtt utility tries to
                             align _s_t_a_r_t offset and partition _s_i_z_e to be mul‐
                             tiple of _a_l_i_g_n_m_e_n_t value.

               --bb _s_t_a_r_t      The logical block address where the partition
                             will begin.  An SI unit suffix is allowed.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s      Additional operational flags.  See the section
                             entitled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                             about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x      The index in the partition table at which the new
                             partition is to be placed.  The index determines
                             the name of the device special file used to rep‐
                             resent the partition.

               --ll _l_a_b_e_l      The label attached to the partition.  This option
                             is only valid when used on partitioning schemes
                             that support partition labels.

               --ss _s_i_z_e       Create a partition of size _s_i_z_e.  An SI unit suf‐
                             fix is allowed.

               --tt _t_y_p_e       Create a partition of type _t_y_p_e.  Partition types
                             are discussed below in the section entitled
                             _P_A_R_T_I_T_I_O_N _T_Y_P_E_S.

     bbaacckkuupp    Dump a partition table to standard output in a special format
               used by the rreessttoorree action.

     bboooottccooddee  Embed bootstrap code into the partitioning scheme's metadata on
               the _g_e_o_m (using --bb _b_o_o_t_c_o_d_e) or write bootstrap code into a
               partition (using --pp _p_a_r_t_c_o_d_e and --ii _i_n_d_e_x).

               The bboooottccooddee command accepts these options:

               --NN          Do not preserve the Volume Serial Number for MBR.
                           MBR bootcode contains Volume Serial Number by de‐
                           fault, and ggppaarrtt tries to preserve it when in‐
                           stalling new bootstrap code.  This option skips
                           preservation to help with some versions of
                           boot0cfg(8) that do not support Volume Serial Num‐
                           ber.

               --bb _b_o_o_t_c_o_d_e
                           Embed bootstrap code from the file _b_o_o_t_c_o_d_e into
                           the partitioning scheme's metadata for _g_e_o_m.  Not
                           all partitioning schemes have embedded bootstrap
                           code, so the --bb _b_o_o_t_c_o_d_e option is scheme-specific
                           in nature (see the section entitled _B_O_O_T_S_T_R_A_P_P_I_N_G
                           below).  The _b_o_o_t_c_o_d_e file must match the parti‐
                           tioning scheme's requirements for file content and
                           size.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x    Specify the target partition for --pp _p_a_r_t_c_o_d_e.

               --pp _p_a_r_t_c_o_d_e
                           Write the bootstrap code from the file _p_a_r_t_c_o_d_e
                           into the _g_e_o_m partition specified by --ii _i_n_d_e_x.  The
                           size of the file must be smaller than the size of
                           the partition.

     ccoommmmiitt    Commit any pending changes for geom _g_e_o_m.  All actions are com‐
               mitted by default and will not result in pending changes.  Ac‐
               tions can be modified with the --ff _f_l_a_g_s option so that they are
               not committed, but become pending.  Pending changes are re‐
               flected by the geom and the ggppaarrtt utility, but they are not ac‐
               tually written to disk.  The ccoommmmiitt action will write all pend‐
               ing changes to disk.

     ccrreeaattee    Create a new partitioning scheme on a provider given by
               _p_r_o_v_i_d_e_r.  The scheme to use must be specified with the --ss
               _s_c_h_e_m_e option.

               The ccrreeaattee command accepts these options:

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --nn _e_n_t_r_i_e_s  The number of entries in the partition table.  Ev‐
                           ery partitioning scheme has a minimum and maximum
                           number of entries.  This option allows tables to be
                           created with a number of entries that is within the
                           limits.  Some schemes have a maximum equal to the
                           minimum and some schemes have a maximum large
                           enough to be considered unlimited.  By default,
                           partition tables are created with the minimum num‐
                           ber of entries.

               --ss _s_c_h_e_m_e   Specify the partitioning scheme to use.  The kernel
                           must have support for a particular scheme before
                           that scheme can be used to partition a disk.

     ddeelleettee    Delete a partition from geom _g_e_o_m and further identified by the
               --ii _i_n_d_e_x option.  The partition cannot be actively used by the
               kernel.

               The ddeelleettee command accepts these options:

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x    Specifies the index of the partition to be deleted.

     ddeessttrrooyy   Destroy the partitioning scheme as implemented by geom _g_e_o_m.

               The ddeessttrrooyy command accepts these options:

               --FF          Forced destroying of the partition table even if it
                           is not empty.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

     mmooddiiffyy    Modify a partition from geom _g_e_o_m and further identified by the
               --ii _i_n_d_e_x option.  Only the type and/or label of the partition
               can be modified.  Not all partitioning schemes support labels
               and it is invalid to try to change a partition label in such
               cases.

               The mmooddiiffyy command accepts these options:

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x    Specifies the index of the partition to be modi‐
                           fied.

               --ll _l_a_b_e_l    Change the partition label to _l_a_b_e_l.

               --tt _t_y_p_e     Change the partition type to _t_y_p_e.

     rreeccoovveerr   Recover a corrupt partition's scheme metadata on the geom _g_e_o_m.
               See the section entitled _R_E_C_O_V_E_R_I_N_G below for the additional
               information.

               The rreeccoovveerr command accepts these options:

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

     rreessiizzee    Resize a partition from geom _g_e_o_m and further identified by the
               --ii _i_n_d_e_x option.  If the new size is not specified it is auto‐
               matically calculated to be the maximum available from _g_e_o_m.

               The rreessiizzee command accepts these options:

               --aa _a_l_i_g_n_m_e_n_t  If specified, then the ggppaarrtt utility tries to
                             align partition _s_i_z_e to be a multiple of the
                             _a_l_i_g_n_m_e_n_t value.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s      Additional operational flags.  See the section
                             entitled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                             about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x      Specifies the index of the partition to be re‐
                             sized.

               --ss _s_i_z_e       Specifies the new size of the partition, in logi‐
                             cal blocks.  An SI unit suffix is allowed.

     rreessttoorree   Restore the partition table from a backup previously created by
               the bbaacckkuupp action and read from standard input.  Only the par‐
               tition table is restored.  This action does not affect the con‐
               tent of partitions.  After restoring the partition table and
               writing bootcode if needed, user data must be restored from
               backup.

               The rreessttoorree command accepts these options:

               --FF          Destroy partition table on the given _p_r_o_v_i_d_e_r be‐
                           fore doing restore.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --ll          Restore partition labels for partitioning schemes
                           that support them.

     sseett       Set the named attribute on the partition entry.  See the sec‐
               tion entitled _A_T_T_R_I_B_U_T_E_S below for a list of available at‐
               tributes.

               The sseett command accepts these options:

               --aa _a_t_t_r_i_b   Specifies the attribute to set.

               --ff _f_l_a_g_s    Additional operational flags.  See the section en‐
                           titled _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N_A_L _F_L_A_G_S below for a discussion
                           about its use.

               --ii _i_n_d_e_x    Specifies the index of the partition on which the
                           attribute will be set.

     sshhooww      Show current partition information for the specified geoms, or
               all geoms if none are specified.  The default output includes
               the logical starting block of each partition, the partition
               size in blocks, the partition index number, the partition type,
               and a human readable partition size.  Block sizes and locations
