PPAARRTTIITTIIOONNIINNGG SSCCHHEEMMEESS
     Several partitioning schemes are supported by the ggppaarrtt utility:

     AAPPMM    Apple Partition Map, used by PowerPC(R) Macintosh(R) computers.
            Requires the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__AAPPMM kernel option.

     BBSSDD    Traditional BSD disklabel(8), usually used to subdivide MBR parti‐
            tions.  (This scheme can also be used as the sole partitioning
            method, without an MBR.  Partition editing tools from other
            operating systems often do not understand the bare disklabel
            partition layout, so this is sometimes called “dangerously
            dedicated”.)  Requires the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__BBSSDD kernel option.

     BBSSDD6644  64-bit implementation of BSD disklabel used in DragonFly to subdi‐
            vide MBR or GPT partitions.  Requires the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__BBSSDD6644 kernel
            option.

     LLDDMM    The Logical Disk Manager is an implementation of volume manager
            for Microsoft Windows NT.  Requires the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__LLDDMM kernel op‐
            tion.

     GGPPTT    GUID Partition Table is used on Intel-based Macintosh computers
            and gradually replacing MBR on most PCs and other systems.  Re‐
            quires the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__GGPPTT kernel option.

     MMBBRR    Master Boot Record is used on PCs and removable media.  Requires
            the GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__MMBBRR kernel option.  The GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__EEBBRR option adds
            support for the Extended Boot Record (EBR), which is used to de‐
            fine a logical partition.  The GGEEOOMM__PPAARRTT__EEBBRR__CCOOMMPPAATT option enables
            backward compatibility for partition names in the EBR scheme.  It
            also prevents any type of actions on such partitions.

     See glabel(8) for additional information on labelization of devices and
     partitions.

PPAARRTTIITTIIOONN TTYYPPEESS
     Partition types are identified on disk by particular strings or magic
     values.  The ggppaarrtt utility uses symbolic names for common partition types
     so the user does not need to know these values or other details of the
     partitioning scheme in question.  The ggppaarrtt utility also allows the user
     to specify scheme-specific partition types for partition types that do
     not have symbolic names.  Symbolic names currently understood and used by
     FreeBSD are:

     aappppllee--bboooott             The system partition dedicated to storing boot
                            loaders on some Apple systems.  The scheme-spe‐
                            cific types are "!171" for MBR, "!Apple_Bootstrap"
                            for APM, and
                            "!426f6f74-0000-11aa-aa11-00306543ecac" for GPT.

     bbiiooss--bboooott              The system partition dedicated to second stage of
                            the boot loader program.  Usually it is used by
                            the GRUB 2 loader for GPT partitioning schemes.
                            The scheme-specific type is
                            "!21686148-6449-6E6F-744E-656564454649".

     eeffii                    The system partition for computers that use the
                            Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI).  The scheme-
                            specific types are "!239" for MBR, and
                            "!c12a7328-f81f-11d2-ba4b-00a0c93ec93b" for GPT.

     ffrreeeebbssdd                A FreeBSD partition subdivided into filesystems
                            with a BSD disklabel.  This is a legacy partition
                            type and should not be used for the APM or GPT
                            schemes.  The scheme-specific types are "!165" for
                            MBR, "!FreeBSD" for APM, and
                            "!516e7cb4-6ecf-11d6-8ff8-00022d09712b" for GPT.

     ffrreeeebbssdd--bboooott           A FreeBSD partition dedicated to bootstrap code.
                            The scheme-specific type is
                            "!83bd6b9d-7f41-11dc-be0b-001560b84f0f" for GPT.

     ffrreeeebbssdd--sswwaapp           A FreeBSD partition dedicated to swap space.  The
                            scheme-specific types are "!FreeBSD-swap" for APM,
                            and "!516e7cb5-6ecf-11d6-8ff8-00022d09712b" for
                            GPT.

     ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss            A FreeBSD partition that contains a UFS or UFS2
                            filesystem.  The scheme-specific types are
                            "!FreeBSD-UFS" for APM, and
